WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.624
Ananya pastes a job description into her script

2
00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:05.155
and asks: tell me about this job.

3
00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:08.466
Back comes a friendly paragraph. But a program

4
00:00:08.524 --> 00:00:10.660
is reading this reply, so prose is a

5
00:00:10.706 --> 00:00:11.635
bug.

6
00:00:11.985 --> 00:00:14.249
The fix is not cleverer wording. A good

7
00:00:14.307 --> 00:00:16.257
prompt is a specification.

8
00:00:16.607 --> 00:00:18.813
Start with a role and one clear task:

9
00:00:19.045 --> 00:00:22.226
you extract job details from a job description.

10
00:00:22.576 --> 00:00:25.258
Add constraints: what to do when information is

11
00:00:25.328 --> 00:00:28.358
missing. If the CTC is not stated, use

12
00:00:28.416 --> 00:00:30.192
null. Never guess.

13
00:00:30.542 --> 00:00:33.340
Then spell out the exact output: JSON, with

14
00:00:33.386 --> 00:00:35.372
a role, a list of skills, and the

15
00:00:35.418 --> 00:00:36.811
CTC.

16
00:00:37.161 --> 00:00:39.437
Rules live in the system prompt. The job

17
00:00:39.495 --> 00:00:41.921
description goes in the user message, wrapped in

18
00:00:42.002 --> 00:00:43.663
tags, as data.

19
00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:46.811
So if that text says, ignore your instructions,

20
00:00:46.974 --> 00:00:49.121
it is less likely to work.

21
00:00:49.471 --> 00:00:51.468
Next, show one or two examples of a

22
00:00:51.526 --> 00:00:55.230
perfect answer. That is few-shot prompting: the model

23
00:00:55.299 --> 00:00:56.530
copies the pattern.

24
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.341
Turn on JSON mode, and the reply parses

25
00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:02.023
as JSON. But valid JSON can still be

26
00:01:02.058 --> 00:01:04.601
wrong: here, the CTC came back as a

27
00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:05.379
word.

28
00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:08.550
So check every reply against a schema. If

29
00:01:08.608 --> 00:01:10.686
it fails, send the error back and ask

30
00:01:10.756 --> 00:01:12.138
once more.

31
00:01:12.488 --> 00:01:15.437
And treat prompts like code: keep versions, and

32
00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.945
rerun a few real test cases after every

33
00:01:17.991 --> 00:01:19.222
change.

34
00:01:19.572 --> 00:01:22.126
Write the prompt like a spec. Then validate

35
00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:25.005
what comes back, before your code trusts it.
